contentText:
Since 1970, the Nashville Songwriters Hall of Fame has honored more than 250 of Music City&#8217;s top weavers of words and melodies, recognizing their talent, songcraft, and enduring impact on musical culture by welcoming them as members.

On Monday night (Oct. 26), the Nashville Songwriters Hall of Fame welcomed six new members into its ranks, when the 56th annual Nashville Songwriters Hall of Fame Gala was held at Music City Center. Nashville Songwriters Hall of Fame executive director Mark Ford and Board chair Rich Hallworth ushered in the evening by welcoming the audience.

Taylor Swift joined the Hall&#8217;s Class of 2026 in the songwriter-artist category. Her fellow honorees were J.I. Allison (in the legacy category), Bruce Channel (veteran songwriter category), Lyle Lovett (veteran songwriter-artist category), and Shawn Camp and Lee Thomas Miller (both in the songwriter category).

Brad Paisley inducted Swift (&#8220;at your core, you&#8217;re a songwriter,&#8221; Paisley told Swift) while Lainey Wilson honored Swift with a rendition of Swift&#8217;s &#8220;Love Story.&#8221; Swift reflected on the meaning behind the words Nashville, songwriters and fame.

“I knew from the time I wrote my first song that even if it couldn’t be my career, it would be my passion,” she told the audience. “It would be what I centered my entire life around.” She recalled being a teen just starting her career, balancing school and songwriting, and how working with writers and producers including Liz Rose and Nathan Chapman helped her further refine her songwriting skills.

“I think you truly learn a craft when you do it over and over and over,” Swift said. “And that is what the magnificent songwriters that work in this amazing town do.” She said the Nashville&#8217;s songwriters &#8220;in a lot of ways, raised me and guided me through my formative years.&#8221;

Her induction into the Nashville Songwriters Hall of Fame, at a gala held in downtown Nashville, comes just after the 20-year anniversary of the release of Swift&#8217;s debut album, which included her first hits such as &#8220;Tim McGraw,&#8221; &#8220;Picture To Burn&#8221; and &#8220;Teardrops on My Guitar.&#8221; She gave credit to Nashville&#8217;s songwriting community for helping her develop the ability to traverse various musical styles, collaborations and approaches to writing throughout her career.

“The only reason that I’ve been able to have that kind of dexterity in my creative process is because of the fundamentals and the foundation that I learned here in Nashville,” Swift said. “And I never forget that for a single second.”

Journalist/music historian Robert K Oermann recognized Allison, known for penning classic songs such as &#8220;That&#8217;ll Be The Day&#8221; and &#8220;Peggy Sue.&#8221; BR5-49 frontman Chuck Mead performed a rendition of &#8220;That&#8217;ll Be The Day,&#8221; while Joanie Allison accepted the Nashville Songwriters Hall of Fame honor on behalf of her late husband. &#8220;Today is your day,&#8221; she said.

Kix Brooks honored inductee Channel, known for songs including John Conlee&#8217;s &#8220;As Long As I&#8217;m Rockin&#8217; With You.&#8221; Lee Roy Parnell performed a rendition of Channel&#8217;s Billboard Hot 100 hit &#8220;Hey! Baby.&#8221;

&#8220;He was multi-faceted in his writing,&#8221; Brooks recalled of first getting to know Channel. Meanwhile, Channel thanked many in the Nashville community for their contributions to his career, including music publishing executive Don Gant, saying, &#8220;He helped everybody he came across,&#8221; and recalling how Gant helped him get the song &#8220;Party Time&#8221; recorded by T.G. Sheppard; the song became a No. 1 country hit.

The evening also saw George Strait honored with the career maker award. During Strait&#8217;s more than four decades in the country music spotlight, he has recorded 264 songs written or co-written by 72 Nashville Songwriters Hall of Fame members—44 of whom were not in the Hall when he first cut their songs.

After members of the Nashville Songwriters Hall of Fame had taken the stage, Strait joined them, led by Dean Dillon, and thanked them for their music.

&#8220;First of all, congratulations to the new 2026 Nashville Songwriters Hall of Fame inductees,&#8221; Strait said, before going on to note that he felt that receiving an accolade called a career-maker award seemed &#8220;turned around&#8230;&#8217;cause they made my career. I didn&#8217;t make theirs. That&#8217;s the way I see it&#8230;Nashville has shown me a lot of love over the years&#8230;I&#8217;ve always loved coming to Nashville, being here and recording here and being around these guys. I just want to say thank all of you who send songs my way and made my career. I appreciate it very much.&#8221;

Nashville Songwriters Hall of Fame songwriter Pat Alger presented Lovett as one of the Class of 2026 inductees, describing his music &#8220;uniquely Lyle Lovett songs&#8230;As a songwriter, that&#8217;s something you want people to say about you.&#8221; Kathy Mattea performed a version of &#8220;If I Had A Boat,&#8221; a song that exemplifies Lovett&#8217;s vivid songwriting and eclectic musical style. Lovett is also known for songs including &#8220;Cowboy Man,&#8221; &#8220;Why I Don&#8217;t Know&#8221; and the Grammy-nominated &#8220;She&#8217;s No Lady.&#8221; Lovett expressed gratitude for the honor, while also thanking his peers, but also his parents for their steady support, saying, &#8220;They gave me every opportunity they could.&#8221;

Verlon Thompson and Emmylou Harris honored Camp with a performance, while Jim Lauderdale inducted Camp, known for songs including &#8220;How Long Gone&#8221; (recorded by Brooks & Dunn), &#8220;Would You Go With Me&#8221; (Josh Turner), &#8220;My Love Will Not Change&#8221; (The Del McCoury Band) and &#8220;River of Love&#8221; (Strait).

&#8220;Unbelievable,&#8221; Camp said after taking the stage, soaking in the moment. He called Lauderdale one of his songwriting heroes, and noted that the honor was as much about anyone that had ever co-written with him as it was about Camp. He also honored the late John Anderson, saying he was &#8220;always going above and beyond to help me in this business.&#8221;

The evening&#8217;s final inductee was Lee Thomas Miller, known for his work on songs including &#8220;The Impossible,&#8221; &#8220;You&#8217;re Gonna Miss This,&#8221; &#8220;In Color,&#8221; &#8220;It Ain&#8217;t My Fault,&#8221; and &#8220;Southern Girl.&#8221; Jamey Johnson performed a powerful acoustic rendition of &#8220;In Color,&#8221; while Alabama&#8217;s Randy Owen presented Miller as a member of the newest Nashville Songwriters Hall of Fame class.

Miller offered up one of the evening&#8217;s most powerful speeches, congratulating his fellow inductees and calling it a &#8220;great honor to share this night with you.&#8221; His speech focused on honoring his family, his co-writers and those who, like Miller, advocate in Washington, D.C. for the rights of songwriters. He took listeners through his journey of how loving baseball as a child evolved into finding his creative light in music. Most of all, he encouraged the songwriter community, saying, &#8220;These are the people who write the songs. These are the people who make the world make sense.&#8221;

Even as the Nashville Songwriters Hall of Fame Gala ushered in a new class of inductees, the evening also took time to honor those who have passed away since last year&#8217;s event. There was a tribute to 650 WSM AM&#8217;s Bill Cody, the gala&#8217;s longtime announcer, who passed away in June. Grand Ole Opry announcer Kelly Sutton stepped in for Cody as the show&#8217;s &#8220;voice&#8221; for the evening. The segment also remembered eight Nashville Songwriters Hall of Fame songwriters who have passed away since last year&#8217;s ceremony, including Walt Aldridge, John Anderson, Rory Bourke, Steve Cropper, Jim McBride, Joe M…
